ation's  highest executive office and the  chairmanship of a private corporation  that's engaged in raising investment for  massive development project that would  increase the value of his own property  many times over now I'm talking of  course about George Washington into the  early months of his presidency he  continued to lead the Potomac Company  which had been organized for the  commercial development of the Potomac  River with a view to making it into the  country's  main thoroughfare into the westward into  the West Country to the interior of  North America its development would also  dramatically increased the value of  Washington's vast land holdings in  western Pennsylvania and present-day  West Virginia but in 1789 it's hardly  anyone batted an eye  not so much because Washington was  beyond reproach though he pretty much  was but because what we call conflict of  interest was rarely perceived as  politically scandalous in the early days  of the Republic Washington wasn't alone  most of the government's leading men  held potentially very lucrative  investments that would benefit from  legislation they actively and often  openly promoted so then just just what's  a scandal at least in 1790 is something  is scandal because the behavior involved  is inherently illegal immoral unethical  or unseemly by today's standards or by  our ancestors standards this brief look  at what scandalized our forebears of the  1790s will show us something about how  our notion of what constitutes a public  or a political scandal has changed and  why what shocks us today may have  shocked almost no one then and by the  same token why much of what scandalized  them means little to us now there's no  better place to begin than the masa  nations that surrounded the  establishment of the national capital or  as it was then called the federal city  many different sites more than 30 were  under consideration from Kingston New  York to Norfolk Virginia but the main  contenders were in New York City the  home of the federal government at the  time several sites in Pennsylvania and  on the Potomac regional advocates  lobbied ferociously for their favorite  locations among these the boldest  example was Pennsylvania senator Robert  Morris who bought up land at every  proposed site near Philadelphia and  battled hard to place the capital on  any one of them his fellow senator from  Pennsylvania William McClay the two  hated each other by the way  William McClay worked equally hard to  win the Capitol for a site on the  Susquehanna River where he and his  political allies owned land after a  sequence of horse trading backroom deals  so many of them that I I wouldn't dare  begin to recount them for you Congress  finally settled on the Potomac but where  on the Potomac Congress deputed George  Washington to pick the best spot it was  generally expected that he would select  a site far upriver where Maryland is  narrowest so that the federal district  might eventually encompass parts of  three states Pennsylvania Maryland and  Virginia  instead he insisted on the present site  most of which lay outside the zone  mandated by Congress and required  Congress to vote again to enlarge that  zone to accommodate him the site he  chose as we all know lies conveniently  across the river from his property at  Mount Vernon after Washington's death  John Adams tartly declared that the deal  raised the value of Washington's  properties a thousand percent the  president also raised the rents on his  lands in the area as soon as Congress  approved the site he then named three of  his friends all principals of the  Potomac Company as commissioners to  manage the development of the capital  now in today's terms Washington's  actions obviously represented a gross  conflict of interest but no one even  suggested censoring him nothing he did  was illegal at the time nor did any  congressional committees exist to  investigate it even if it had been in  this Washington was 