is position in an attempt to  corner at the New York bond market  cornered by creditors he was reduced to  borrowing not just from banks and fellow  finance seers but from shopkeepers  widows  and even one of the city's better known  Madame's he failed in March 1792 Dewar's  collapse brought on the nation's first  Wall Street Crash bankrupting many of  the city's biggest money men including  James Wilson a Justice of the Supreme  Court who was imprisoned for debt as a  result of failed speculations and spent  the rest of his life running from  creditors Dewar himself was financially  ruined almost lynched and jailed in  debtors prison from which he never  emerged the cash reserves of the Bank of  the United States plummeted by 34  percent prompting the bank to call in a  quarter of its outstanding loans now bad  as this was it would have been a great  deal worse without the cook without  quick work by Alexander Hamilton who  acted to stabilize the securities market  and staved off a full-scale recession  interest rates soared to a ruinous 1% a  day Hamilton wrote his time there must  be a line of separation between honest  men and knaves between respectable  stockholders and dealers in the funds  and more unprincipled gamblers now  Americans would still be saying much the  same thing two centuries later meanwhile  back on the Potomac the money upon which  Samuel Blodgett you remember him  Samuel Blodgett along with the president  the commissioners in Congress had pinned  their hopes upon evaporated into thin  air scarcely anyone was interested in  buying land in the federal district and  of those who did many refused to pay the  only real winner was Blodgett himself  who acquired at a secretly discounted  price the most valuable piece of real  estate on offer  since land sale seemed unpromising  Blodgett proposed another scheme raising  money through a lottery with three  hundred and fifty thousand dollars in  prizes including a non-existent hotel  it's true I kid you not once again  pelagic Blodgett succeeded in gulling  the commissioners as well as Washington  and Jefferson when this two failed to  generate any income  the commissioners finally begged  Blodgett to stop the business as soon as  possible by 1793  construction of the government buildings  had almost completely stalled the  federal city looked set to become a  spectacular national failure with  serious consequences for the frail young  Republic if the buildings were not  completed at the end of the ten-year  deadline set by Congress that's November  1800 the Capitol would remain very  likely permanently in Philadelphia at  this dire moment three new financed ears  with impeccable credentials rode to the  rescue James Greenleaf of Boston who had  made a fortune in land speculation John  Nicholson Pennsylvania's respected  Comptroller General and US Senator  Robert Morris who having failed to win  the Capitol for Pennsylvania now staked  his fortune and reputation on cornering  the real estate in the federal district  they bought out Blodgett and promised to  succeed where he had failed the economic  historian Am Sikorsky writing in the  1930s would call them the greatest  land-grabbing triumvirate that ever  operated in America the partners  contracted with the commissioners to buy  thousands of Lots in the federal city  for over a million dollars they  committed themselves to erect a house  and every third locked into paying the  commissioners annual installments over  the next seven years until the project  was completed in all they controlled 42  percent of the available real estate in  the district to pay for it they would  float a 1.2 million dollar loan a  stupendous sum at the time from lenders  in Europe so from here on the capital's  fate essentially became the prisoner of  a speculative scheme so gargantuan and  tangled that it still defies attempts to  fully unravel it  essentially the three partners created a  pyramid scheme its collapse literally  sh