d by liens and litigation  would be investors regarded the places  of failure in the end grudging  appropriations by Congress in the state  of Maryland provided enough money for  the government buildings to be completed  by the 1800 deadline but just barely  you could still smell the paint inside  the walls of the Capitol building as  Congress arrived it would be generations  before Washington grew into anything  like the Grand National Capitol that the  founders had imagined so I asked what  was the difference between George  Washington's transparent conflicts of  interest and Robert Morris's  speculations now a cynic might say  merely that one man's scheme succeeded  and the others failed and that winners  don't have to justify themselves of  course gratitude to Washington as the  Paramount hero of the Revolutionary War  meant that in fact he could do almost no  wrong in the public eye at least in the  early days of his presidency even John  Adams regarded his transgressions if  that's what they were as unseemly rather  than illicit  even Mars and Nicholson like doer before  them were not prosecuted for fraud or  insider trading  they were jailed for indebtedness now  honor is it word rarely heard in public  life in our own day but it was a quality  that men of the 18th century rated among  the most sterling and essential of human  virtues a man's honor could be breached  or enhanced in many ways but one of the  surest ways to lose it to become  dishonored was to fall into debt which  entailed the betrayal of one's creditors  and we should bear in mind that many  innocent people were ruined financially  as a result of these speculators schemes  I'll mention again that did in 1796  James Wilson remember the Supreme Court  was twice imprisoned for death  up to now I've been talking only about  financial misbehavior but what about the  more personal kinds of scandal misdeeds  of the boudoir so to speak or in plain  language sex scandals the looms so large  on our own